---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---
<Layout title="Security" path="/security/" description="SentinelCore security model: privilege separation, role-based access, local-only data, and exactly what the email relay does and does not see.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Security model</p>
      <h1>Security</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        SentinelCore is built around one idea: concentrate privilege in one small, auditable
        place, and keep everything else powerless.
      </p>

      <h2>Privilege separation</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <Card title="Powerless API">
          The backend and pipeline worker run as an unprivileged user with <code>cap_drop: ALL</code>
          and <code>no-new-privileges</code>. They can't touch raw sockets or the firewall.
        </Card>
        <Card title="One capability boundary">
          The <code>helper</code> holds only <code>NET_RAW</code>, <code>NET_ADMIN</code>,
          <code>SYS_NICE</code> and <code>CHOWN</code> — never full privilege — and is reached only
          over a <code>0660 root:sentinelcore</code> Unix socket.
        </Card>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">Role-based access</h2>
      <p>
        Accounts are Admin or Analyst. Passwords are hashed with Argon2; access tokens are short-lived;
        the refresh cookie is scoped to <code>/api/auth</code> and marked <code>Secure</code> in
        production; repeated failed logins are throttled per username + IP.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">What stays local</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-3" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <Card title="Your traffic">Captured frames, flows and uploaded PCAPs never leave the host.</Card>
        <Card title="Your findings">Events, incidents, intel matches and reports live only in your local Postgres and volumes.</Card>
        <Card title="Your config">Secrets are generated at install and stored in a <code>600</code> .env on your box.</Card>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">What the email relay does — and doesn't — see</h2>
      <Callout tone="ok" title="The relay never holds your data">
        SentinelCore can send notifications through a central relay so that no mail-provider key
        ever ships to your box. The relay only ever receives: the <em>recipient</em> (your verified
        admin address), a <em>subject</em>, and the rendered <em>notification body</em> for an
        allow-listed notification type — plus your revocable per-install token for authentication.
      </Callout>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <Card title="The relay DOES see">
          <ul>
            <li>Your admin email address (the only allowed recipient)</li>
            <li>The subject and body of notifications you choose to send</li>
            <li>A per-install token (a hash is stored; it's revocable any time)</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="The relay does NOT see">
          <ul>
            <li>Any raw network traffic, PCAPs or flows</li>
            <li>Your events, incidents or reports database</li>
            <li>Any provider API key on your side — there isn't one on your box</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>

      <Callout tone="danger" title="Lab / authorized-network use">
        SentinelCore actively scans and can block hosts on the network you point it at. Run it only
        on networks you are authorized to monitor and defend.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; color: rgb(var(--text-muted)); }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
</style>
